window.docContents[56].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-automorphism-group-of-the-hypercube":{"contents":"As a substantial application of the bridge we identify the automorphism group of the\nhypercube: it is the hyperoctahedral group, the wreath product ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ of order\n2 ^ n * n!. Concretely every automorphism of the hypercube is uniquely a\ncomposition of a translation (xor by a fixed vertex) and a\ncoordinate permutation, and the wreath product is the semidirect product\n(ℤ/2)ⁿ ⋊ Sₙ that records how coordinate permutations act on translations.\n\nWe work in the Hamming model and transport the result to Q[n] at the end. The\nproof has four steps.\n\n1. Translations and coordinate permutations are automorphisms of the Hamming cube.2. Together they give a group homomorphism from (ℤ/2)ⁿ ⋊ Sₙ to the Hamming cube's\nautomorphism group.3. This homomorphism is injective: evaluate at 0 and at the standard basis vectors\neᵢ. (The standard basis vectors are given by Pi.single in Lean so our informal\neᵢ refers to Pi.single i true. We introduce the notation e i below.)4. It is surjective. After composing with a translation, an automorphism fixes 0.\nIt then preserves Hamming weight, so it permutes the basis vectors, which gives a\npermutation σ. Undoing σ leaves an automorphism fixing 0 and every eᵢ,\nwhich is the identity by a rigidity argument: the coordinates of a vertex can be\nread off from its distances to 0 and to the eᵢ.\n\nThe key to making this precise in Lean is that Mathlib makes Bool a BooleanRing,\nwith 0 = false, 1 = true and addition given by xor. Consequently the vertex\ntype Fin n → Bool of the Hamming cube is an additive group, namely (ℤ/2)ⁿ, with\npointwise xor (^^) as addition: translations are simply (t + ·).\n\nexample {n : ℕ} : AddCommGroup (Fin n → Bool) := inferInstance\nexample (a b : Bool) : a + b = (a ^^ b) := rfl\n\n\nAddition on Bool vectors has characteristic two: negation is the identity and\nadding the same vector twice gives 0. Mathlib proves both facts in any Boolean\nring, as BooleanRing.neg_eq and BooleanRing.add_self, but it has no instance\nmaking a product of Boolean rings a Boolean ring, so they do not apply to\nFin n → Bool directly. The missing instance is easy: Mathlib already provides\nthe pointwise ring structure on functions, and multiplication is idempotent because\nit is idempotent in each coordinate.\n\nA product of Boolean rings is a Boolean ring under the pointwise operations./-- A product of Boolean rings is a Boolean ring under the pointwise operations. -/\ninstance _root_.Pi.instBooleanRing {ι : Type*} {R : ι → Type*}\n    [∀ i, BooleanRing (R i)] : BooleanRing (∀ i, R i) where\n  isIdempotentElem a := funext fun i => BooleanRing.mul_self (a i)\n\n\nThe two lemmas are simp lemmas only once the BooleanRing namespace is open\n(they are scoped), so we open it for the rest of the chapter with\nopen scoped BooleanRing. Mathlib has no lemma for x + (x + y) = y, so we add\none.\n\nopen scoped BooleanRing\n\nexample {n : ℕ} (x : Fin n → Bool) : -x = x := by simp\nexample {n : ℕ} (x : Fin n → Bool) : x + x = 0 := by simp\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma boolVec_add_add_cancel {n : ℕ} (x y : Fin n → Bool) : x + (x + y) = y := by\n  rw [← add_assoc, BooleanRing.add_self, zero_add]\n\n\nThe vectors of Hamming weight (hammingNorm) one are exactly the standard basis\nvectors Pi.single i true (all coordinates false except the i-th). For the rest\nof the chapter we write e i for Pi.single i true, our informal eᵢ. The notation\nis scoped to hypercubeHamming, so later files can use it with\nopen scoped hypercubeHamming.\n\nscoped[SimpleGraph.hypercubeHamming] notation:lead \"e \" i:max => Pi.single i true\n\n\nopen scoped hypercubeHamming\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hammingNorm_single {n : ℕ} (i : Fin n) :\n    hammingNorm (e i : Fin n → Bool) = 1 := by\n  simp [hammingNorm, Pi.single_apply, Finset.filter_eq']\n\n\nlemma hammingNorm_eq_one_iff {n : ℕ} {x : Fin n → Bool} :\n    hammingNorm x = 1 ↔ ∃ i, x = e i := by\n  constructor\n  · -- the set of true coordinates of x is a singleton {i}\n    intro hx\n    rw [hammingNorm, Finset.card_eq_one] at hx\n    obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ := hx\n    refine ⟨i, funext fun j => ?_⟩\n    -- so x j = true exactly when j = i\n    have hj : x j = true ↔ j = i := by\n      simpa [Bool.zero_eq_false] using Finset.ext_iff.mp hi j\n    by_cases hji : j = i\n    · simpa [hji] using hj\n    · simpa [hji, Bool.zero_eq_false] using hj\n  · -- conversely, a basis vector has weight one\n    rintro ⟨i, rfl⟩\n    exact hammingNorm_single i\n\n\nAdjacency in the Hamming cube has a clean algebraic description: x and y are\nadjacent iff they differ by a basis vector.\n\nlemma hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists {n : ℕ} {x y : Fin n → Bool} :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).Adj x y ↔ ∃ i, y = x + e i := by\n  rw [hypercubeHamming_adj, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm,\n    BooleanRing.neg_eq, hammingNorm_eq_one_iff]\n  constructor\n  · rintro ⟨i, hi⟩\n    refine ⟨i, ?_⟩\n    rw [← hi, boolVec_add_add_cancel]\n  · rintro ⟨i, rfl⟩\n    exact ⟨i, boolVec_add_add_cancel _ _⟩\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory","header":"12.6. The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-automorphism-group-of-the-hypercube"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-minimum-and-maximum-degree":{"contents":"For a finite graph we can talk about the minimum degree δ(G) and maximum degree\nΔ(G). In Mathlib these are G.minDegree and G.maxDegree. Both require\nFintype V and DecidableRel G.Adj, and (very reasonably) default to 0 if G has\nno vertices.\n\nA graph with vertices of minimum and maximum degree marked\n\n#source minDegree  -- def minDegree [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n                   --   WithTop.untopD 0 (univ.image fun v => G.degree v).min\n\n\n#source maxDegree  -- def maxDegree [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n                   --   WithBot.unbotD 0 (univ.image fun v => G.degree v).max\n\n\nWe introduce the standard notation δ(G) for the minimum degree and Δ(G) for the\nmaximum degree of the graph G.\n\n@[inherit_doc minDegree] scoped notation \"δ(\" G \")\" => SimpleGraph.minDegree G\n@[inherit_doc maxDegree] scoped notation \"Δ(\" G \")\" => SimpleGraph.maxDegree G\n\n\nEvery vertex has degree at least the minimum degree and at most the maximum degree.\n\n#check minDegree_le_degree  -- δ(G) ≤ G.degree v\n#check degree_le_maxDegree  -- G.degree v ≤ Δ(G)\n\n\nIf G is a subgraph of H then δ(G) ≤ δ(H).\n\n#check minDegree_le_minDegree -- G ≤ H → δ(G) ≤ δ(H)\n\n\nThe junk value 0 has a cost when we go the other way. If every vertex has degree at\nleast k then k ≤ δ(G), but only when V is nonempty. Likewise a vertex of\nminimum (or maximum) degree exists only when V is nonempty. The upper bound for\nΔ(G) needs no such hypothesis, since the junk value 0 satisfies it anyway.\n\n#check le_minDegree_of_forall_le_degree -- [Nonempty V] (k : ℕ) :\n                                        --   (∀ v, k ≤ G.degree v) → k ≤ δ(G)\n#check maxDegree_le_of_forall_degree_le -- (k : ℕ) : (∀ v, G.degree v ≤ k) → Δ(G) ≤ k\n#check exists_minimal_degree_vertex     -- [Nonempty V] : ∃ v, δ(G) = G.degree v\n#check exists_maximal_degree_vertex     -- [Nonempty V] : ∃ v, Δ(G) = G.degree v\n\n\nOne problem with the definitions of minDegree and maxDegree is that they require\nFintype V, but plenty of graphs have finite minimum and maximum degree without\nhaving finite vertex sets. For example the infinite path graph pathNat₁ has minimum\ndegree 1 and maximum degree 2 but we can't state these facts using minDegree\nand maxDegree (because pathNat₁ has infinitely many vertices).\n\nWe can however characterize the neighbourhoods and degrees of vertices in pathNat₁.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma neighborFinset_pathNat₁ (n : ℕ) [Fintype (pathNat₁.neighborSet n)] :\n    pathNat₁.neighborFinset n = if n = 0 then {1} else {n - 1, n + 1} := by\n  ext x; cases n <;> simp [eq_comm, or_comm]\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma degree_pathNat₁ (n : ℕ) [Fintype (pathNat₁.neighborSet n)] :\n    pathNat₁.degree n = if n = 0 then 1 else 2 := by\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, neighborFinset_pathNat₁]\n  -- the split_ifs tactic is a convenient way to handle if statements\n  split_ifs <;> grind\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Degrees","header":"4.3.4. Minimum and Maximum Degree","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-minimum-and-maximum-degree"}});